                <pb facs="0333" n="245" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv02/pg245"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72119/2969: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Bulgaria</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sofia</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-12-10T12:00:00+02:00"
                            >December 10, 1918—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received December 12—12:40
                        p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>33 [<hi rendition="#italic">3?</hi>]. Reports have reached me from French,
                    English, Greek and American Red Cross sources that Bulgarian officials are
                    rendering repatriation of Greeks as difficult as possible and subject them to
                    great and unnecessary hardships making them pay railroad fare and other
                    expenses. Another report less well confirmed as coming only from Greek sources
                    is that Bulgarians in emigrating eastern Macedonia are taking with them all
                    horses, cattle, flocks, et cetera, which is in distinct violation of terms of
                    armistice. French commander in chief whom I saw in Salonica and who is expected
                    here shortly told me that he intends to speak energetically to the Bulgarian
                    Government regarding these two points. Above repeated to Paris.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
